# The four Bell states and two-qubit entanglement, the heart of quantum correlation, nonlocality, and quantum
# information, modeled and proved by the kernel. A Bell state is a maximally entangled pair of qubits: the two outcomes
# of a joint measurement are not independent but perfectly tied together. For the state phi-plus, measuring both qubits
# in the same basis ALWAYS gives equal outcomes (both off or both on); for psi-minus, ALWAYS opposite outcomes. Neither
# qubit alone has a definite value before measurement, yet the moment one is measured the partner is fixed. That forced
# agreement is what Bell called nonlocal: no message passes, but the results cannot be reproduced by any pair of values
# fixed in advance. This file models the OUTCOME CORRELATION (which is discrete and exactly checkable), not the complex
# amplitudes. It CONSTRUCTS the four Bell states and the measurement outcomes, COMPUTES the forced partner outcome, and
# PROVES the defining facts: phi-plus is perfectly correlated, psi-minus is perfectly anticorrelated, the correlation is
# an involution (knowing the partner recovers the original outcome, the shared link is consistent), and a phase/parity
# signature genuinely separates the four states. This is the combinatorial core of entanglement: the discrete shadow the
# amplitudes cast on measurement, the part a classical recipe provably cannot fake.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

# A measurement outcome of one qubit, off or on (the zero or one of a single binary measurement).
form bit
  case off
  case on

# The four Bell states, the maximally entangled two-qubit basis. The phi states correlate the outcomes (equal); the psi
# states anticorrelate them (opposite). The plus and minus mark the relative phase, which a parity signature will read.
form bell
  case phi-plus
  case phi-minus
  case psi-plus
  case psi-minus

# Flip a bit, off to on and on to off. The anticorrelated partner is the flip of the measured outcome.
task flip
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read b
    case off
      send back
        make on
    case on
      send back
        make off

# Whether a Bell state correlates its outcomes (the phi states, equal outcomes) or anticorrelates them (the psi states,
# opposite outcomes). This splits the four states into the two correlation classes, ignoring the relative phase.
task is-correlated
  take s, like bell
  like flag
  fork case, read s
    case phi-plus
      send back
        make yes
    case phi-minus
      send back
        make yes
    case psi-plus
      send back
        make no
    case psi-minus
      send back
        make no

# The forced partner outcome: given a Bell state and ONE qubit's measured outcome, the outcome the other qubit MUST show
# when measured in the same basis. For a correlated (phi) state the partner equals the outcome; for an anticorrelated
# (psi) state it is the flip. This is the perfect correlation itself, computed: one measurement fixes the other.
task partner
  take s, like bell
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, call is-correlated, read s
    case yes
      send back
        read b
    case no
      send back
        call flip
          read b

# TEMPLATE: one relative-phase entry. The plus states (phi-plus, psi-plus) carry phase off; the minus states carry phase
# on. This `phase` signature, together with the correlation class, names the Bell state uniquely.
tree phase-of
  take state
  take value

  hook fuse
    case {state}
      send back
        make {value}

# The relative phase of a Bell state, off for the plus states and on for the minus states. With is-correlated it forms
# the two-bit address (correlation, phase) that distinguishes all four states, the way two parity checks label a syndrome.
task phase
  take s, like bell
  like bit
  fork case, read s
    fuse phase-of, phi-plus, off
    fuse phase-of, phi-minus, on
    fuse phase-of, psi-plus, off
    fuse phase-of, psi-minus, on

# PHI-PLUS IS PERFECTLY CORRELATED: partner phi-plus b = b, for every outcome b. Measuring one qubit of phi-plus forces
# the other to the SAME value, so the two results always agree. Proved by induction over the outcome.
rule phi-plus-is-perfectly-correlated
  mark b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call partner
        make phi-plus
        read b
      read b
  fold b

# PSI-MINUS IS PERFECTLY ANTICORRELATED: partner psi-minus b = flip b, for every outcome b. Measuring one qubit of
# psi-minus forces the other to the OPPOSITE value, so the two results always disagree. This is the singlet correlation,
# the one that violates the classical bound the hardest. Proved by induction over the outcome.
rule psi-minus-is-perfectly-anticorrelated
  mark b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call partner
        make psi-minus
        read b
      call flip
        read b
  fold b

# THE CORRELATION IS AN INVOLUTION (correlated case): partner phi-plus (partner phi-plus b) = b, for every b. Knowing the
# partner's outcome and applying the same correlation recovers the original outcome. The shared link is consistent and
# symmetric: it does not matter which qubit is measured first. Proved by induction over the outcome.
rule correlated-partner-is-an-involution
  mark b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call partner
        make phi-plus
        call partner
          make phi-plus
          read b
      read b
  fold b

# THE CORRELATION IS AN INVOLUTION (anticorrelated case): partner psi-minus (partner psi-minus b) = b, for every b. Two
# opposite flips cancel, so the anticorrelated link is equally consistent: recovering the partner of the partner returns
# the original outcome. Together with the correlated case this is the full consistency of the shared correlation: whoever
# measures first, both qubits agree on the same joint result. Proved by induction over the outcome.
rule anticorrelated-partner-is-an-involution
  mark b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call partner
        make psi-minus
        call partner
          make psi-minus
          read b
      read b
  fold b

# A CONCRETE DISTINCTION OF CORRELATION CLASS: phi-plus is correlated (yes) and psi-minus is anticorrelated (no), so the
# two states carry genuinely different correlations. A single shared recipe could not give equal outcomes here and
# opposite outcomes there for the same measurement, which is why these are physically distinct entangled states.
rule phi-plus-is-correlated
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-correlated
        make phi-plus
      make yes
  calm hold

rule psi-minus-is-anticorrelated
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-correlated
        make psi-minus
      make no
  calm hold

# THE PHASE SIGNATURE SEPARATES STATES OF THE SAME CORRELATION CLASS: phi-plus and phi-minus are both correlated, yet
# their relative phase differs (off against on). With is-correlated, the pair (correlation, phase) is the two-bit address
# that names each of the four Bell states uniquely, so no two of them are the same correlation. Witnessed by computation.
rule phi-plus-has-plus-phase
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call phase
        make phi-plus
      make off
  calm hold

rule phi-minus-has-minus-phase
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call phase
        make phi-minus
      make on
  calm hold

# THE FULL TWO-BIT ADDRESS OF PSI-PLUS: anticorrelated (no) with plus phase (off). Read alongside the addresses above,
# the four states map to the four distinct pairs (yes,off), (yes,on), (no,off), (no,on), so the four Bell states are
# completely told apart by these two discrete checks alone, without ever touching the amplitudes.
rule psi-plus-is-anticorrelated-plus-phase
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-correlated
        make psi-plus
      make no
  calm hold
